1
00:00:50,702 --> 00:00:53,816
Good morning, everybody and welcome to Mount Rushmore! 

2
00:00:53,816 --> 00:01:02,525
To start your visit I’m just going to give you a brief account of the history of the memorial before letting you roam about on your own. 

3
00:01:02,525 --> 00:01:04,977
I won’t keep you long. OK?


4
00:01:04,977 --> 00:01:20,249
Mount Rushmore is South Dakota’s top tourist attraction and features the heads of four United States Presidents - George Washington, Thomas Jeﬀerson, Theodore Roosevelt and Abraham Lincoln.

5
00:01:20,249 --> 00:01:27,808
Each head is 18 metres tall, which is taller than the heights of eleven people combined! 

6
00:01:27,808 --> 00:01:39,793
The sculptor initially wanted to depict the presidents from head to waist, but due to a lack of funding the construction had to stop before this vision could be realised.

7
00:01:39,793 --> 00:01:45,170
In total it cost the government $ 1 million to sculpt the heads at Mount Rushmore.


8
00:01:45,170 --> 00:01:54,790
Before the construction of the presidents’ heads, the mountain was just bare rock and forest attracting only a few hikers a year.

9
00:01:54,790 --> 00:02:04,996
The new carving at Mount Rushmore has become an iconic symbol of presidential greatness and has appeared in works of ﬁction and other popular works.

10
00:02:05,071 --> 00:02:16,617
The sculpture has also worked perfectly as a way to develop tourism, which was its intended purpose, and now attracts over  two million people a year.

11
00:02:16,617 --> 00:02:32,323
The original plan was to carve the Presidents’ faces into the granite pillars known as ‘The Needles’, however the sculptor soon realised that these rocks were too eroded and delicate to support such a large sculpture.

12
00:02:32,323 --> 00:02:47,668
Instead he chose to locate the carving at Mount Rushmore due to its grand appearance and brightly lit rock faces that experience maximum exposure to sunlight throughout the day as a result of t h e  south-east orientation.

13
00:02:47,658 --> 00:02:54,918
Upon seeing Mount Rushmore, the sculptor declared ‘America will march along that skyline.’ 


14
00:02:54,918 --> 00:02:59,573
The name of Mount Rushmore also has an interesting history.


15
00:02:59,563 --> 00:03:19,576
The location was originally known as the Six Grandfathers, however during an expedition in 1885 the mountain  was  renamed after Charles  E.   Rushmore, a prominent New York  lawyer who joked that his annual treks to the mountain had earned him the right to have it named after him.

16
00:03:20,062 --> 00:03:31,960
Forty years after the mountain was renamed, Charles E. Rushmore donated $5,000 towards the sculpting of the president’s heads -the largest single contribution.


17
00:03:31,960 --> 00:03:40,758
In  1927  the  construction  work  started  and  years later was complete with no fatalities.


18
00:03:40,758 --> 00:03:52,553
So that’s the history for you. If you’d like any more information, please feel free to ask me questions, or you can soak up the information from our fantastic guide book.


19
00:04:28,275 --> 00:04:36,321
Now I’m going to give you a plan of the site and I’d just like to point out where everything is so that you can explore	everything for yourself.	

20
00:04:36,321 --> 00:04:41,623
We’re currently standing at the entrance, which is marked with the arrow on the map.

21
00:04:41,623 --> 00:04:47,076
If you follow the trail up to our left, you will ﬁnd the information centre.

22
00:04:47,076 --> 00:04:59,797
There’s a great photo booth there where you can have your photograph taken with Mount Rushmore in the background for a fee of  only  $10  -  what  a  great  souvenir!

23
00:04:59,797 --> 00:05:09,934
In front of us is the refreshment centre where you can help yourselves to coﬀee, locally grown tea and a delicious selection of cold drinks and biscuits.

24
00:05:09,934 --> 00:05:19,401
Be sure to stay hydrated as it can get really hot up here! To our right not far up the trail is the gift shop.

25
00:05:19,401 --> 00:05:28,427
Here we sell copies of the guide book and it’s also the perfect place to pick up some small souvenirs for yourself, your family and friends.

26
00:05:28,427 --> 00:05:35,474
Now further up the trail behind the gift shop is a big stone building with a workshop.

27
00:05:35,474 --> 00:05:42,848
This is where all of our souvenirs are made by hand, which you  can purchase in the gift shop like I said before. 

28
00:05:42,848 --> 00:05:48,837
Some  are  even  carved  from pieces of rock taken from Mount Rushmore itself! 

29
00:05:48,837 --> 00:06:02,098
If you carry on walking up  the trail past the workshop you’ll ﬁnd our state of the art visitor centre where you can ﬁnd maps of the walking trails here  at  Mount  Rushmore. 

30
00:06:02,098 --> 00:06:11,882
Now for the real  treat! After you have walked past the visitor centre, you can follow the trail up to  the left, which will take you to our wooden shelter.

31
00:06:11,882 --> 00:06:18,738
From here you will have the best view of Mount Rushmore that there is - an experience not be forgotten!


32
00:06:18,738 --> 00:06:25,133
Right, if anyone wants a guided tour then I’m starting at the information centre. 

33
00:06:25,133 --> 00:06:28,504
If you’d like to follow me, this way please.